1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,386
If your sharpening stone has stopped cutting, you will learn here exactly why, and how to fix it.

2
00:00:06,386 --> 00:00:08,887
It is almost always one of two things.

3
00:00:08,887 --> 00:00:16,788
Either the face is loaded with steel dust and has gone slick, or it has worn hollow in the middle and is no longer flat.

4
00:00:16,788 --> 00:00:18,565
Loading is far more common.

5
00:00:18,565 --> 00:00:24,161
It looks just like a worn out stone, and five minutes with soapy water puts it right.

6
00:00:24,161 --> 00:00:31,469
Dishing is the serious one, because a stone that is not flat cannot grind a flat edge, however careful you are.

7
00:00:31,469 --> 00:00:34,103
Most skaters are never taught this part.

8
00:00:34,103 --> 00:00:40,159
They learn the jig and the passes, then blame their technique when the edge stops coming up.

9
00:00:40,859 --> 00:00:45,771
Do not reach for a flattening plate before you know what you are dealing with.

10
00:00:45,771 --> 00:00:51,501
The two faults feel different on the blade, and the fix for one does nothing for the other.

11
00:00:51,501 --> 00:00:57,672
If the stone feels slick rather than grippy, or leaves a greyish smear on the steel, it is loaded.

12
00:00:57,672 --> 00:00:59,498
Scrub it clean and try again.

13
00:00:59,498 --> 00:01:06,740
If a burr takes far longer than usual to come up, it could be either, so clean it first and then check it for flat.

14
00:01:06,740 --> 00:01:15,052
If light shows under a straight rule laid on the face, or your corners come up rounded however careful you are, the stone is dished.

15
00:01:15,052 --> 00:01:19,900
And if the block flexes, rattles in your hand or has cracked, it is finished.

16
00:01:19,900 --> 00:01:20,593
Replace it.

17
00:01:21,293 --> 00:01:27,785
Loading happens because every pass fills the pores of the stone with the metal it has just removed.

18
00:01:27,785 --> 00:01:33,819
Fine steel particles pack into the surface until the abrasive grains cannot reach the blade.

19
00:01:33,819 --> 00:01:37,754
At that point the stone stops cutting and starts burnishing.

20
00:01:37,754 --> 00:01:40,771
It polishes the steel instead of removing any.

21
00:01:40,771 --> 00:01:47,919
That is why a loaded stone can leave a blade looking brighter while the corners stay just as round as before.

22
00:01:47,919 --> 00:01:49,559
The signs are consistent.

23
00:01:49,559 --> 00:01:52,313
The face feels slippery instead of biting.

24
00:01:52,313 --> 00:01:54,150
It leaves a dull grey smear.

25
00:01:54,150 --> 00:01:59,003
And the burr takes two or three times as long to appear, or never arrives.

26
00:01:59,003 --> 00:02:05,430
If your stone is less than a season old and has gone lifeless, this is almost certainly the cause.

27
00:02:05,430 --> 00:02:07,987
Nothing is wrong with the block itself.

28
00:02:08,687 --> 00:02:10,179
Dishing is uneven wear.

29
00:02:10,179 --> 00:02:18,418
You press hardest through the middle of each stroke and ease off at the ends, so the centre of the stone loses material faster.

30
00:02:18,418 --> 00:02:21,272
Over time the face becomes a shallow hollow.

31
00:02:21,272 --> 00:02:23,931
Look at the two stones here, seen end on.

32
00:02:23,931 --> 00:02:31,521
On the left, a flat stone meets the blade across its full width, and it leaves a flat bottom with two square corners.

33
00:02:31,521 --> 00:02:36,450
On the right, the dished stone only touches the blade at its high shoulders.

34
00:02:36,450 --> 00:02:40,602
Those shoulders take the cut, right at the corners of the blade.

35
00:02:40,602 --> 00:02:47,802
So an hour of careful work gives you a curved bottom and rolled corners, the exact edge you sat down to remove.

36
00:02:48,502 --> 00:02:52,602
Checking for flat takes about ten seconds and no measuring tools.

37
00:02:52,602 --> 00:02:56,512
Lay something you trust to be straight along the working face.

38
00:02:56,512 --> 00:02:59,855
A steel rule or an engineer's straight edge is ideal.

39
00:02:59,855 --> 00:03:02,945
Do not use a blade, however true you think it is.

40
00:03:02,945 --> 00:03:09,567
Short track steel is rockered along its length, so on a perfectly flat stone it shows light at both ends.

41
00:03:09,567 --> 00:03:13,414
That could fool you into flattening a good stone for nothing.

42
00:03:13,414 --> 00:03:18,397
Hold the rule and the stone up against a window or a lamp and look at the join.

43
00:03:18,397 --> 00:03:21,613
Then turn the rule across the width and look again.

44
00:03:21,613 --> 00:03:27,605
Stones wear in both directions, and a face can be true along its length while hollow across it.

45
00:03:28,305 --> 00:03:30,429
Here is what you are looking for.

46
00:03:30,429 --> 00:03:34,807
On the left, no light shows between the straight edge and the stone.

47
00:03:34,807 --> 00:03:37,447
The face is flat, so carry on sharpening.

48
00:03:37,447 --> 00:03:42,662
On the right, a thin sliver of light shows in the middle and closes at both ends.

49
00:03:42,662 --> 00:03:47,619
That face is dished, and it needs flattening before it touches a blade again.

50
00:03:47,619 --> 00:03:54,122
Run this check every second or third sharpening session, and always in the week before a competition.

51
00:03:54,122 --> 00:03:57,019
It is the cheapest insurance in your kit bag.

52
00:03:57,019 --> 00:04:02,491
It also explains a lot of weekends where nothing felt right and nobody could say why.

53
00:04:03,191 --> 00:04:07,596
Cleaning a loaded stone is a five minute job at the kitchen sink.

54
00:04:07,596 --> 00:04:15,728
Wet the face and work it over with a stiff nylon brush or an old toothbrush, along the length and then across the width.

55
00:04:15,728 --> 00:04:19,658
If the grey will not lift, add a little washing up liquid.

56
00:04:19,658 --> 00:04:23,453
Soap helps release metal that water alone leaves behind.

57
00:04:23,453 --> 00:04:31,110
Use a firm nylon pad, not steel wool, because loose steel fibres are the last thing you want in an abrasive face.

58
00:04:31,110 --> 00:04:35,176
Rinse away all the slurry, including what sits at the edges.

59
00:04:35,176 --> 00:04:38,902
Then stand the stone on edge and let it dry completely.

60
00:04:38,902 --> 00:04:42,020
Test it on a blade you are not precious about.

61
00:04:42,020 --> 00:04:49,270
To prevent loading, wipe the face before you start and when you finish, and clear the dust as it builds up.

62
00:04:49,970 --> 00:04:55,881
If the straight edge shows light, bring the face back to level before you sharpen again.

63
00:04:55,881 --> 00:04:57,157
The idea is simple.

64
00:04:57,157 --> 00:05:03,873
Rub the worn face against something flat and more abrasive than the stone, until the hollow is gone.

65
00:05:03,873 --> 00:05:09,515
A diamond plate is the tidiest tool and the most likely to give a truly flat result.

66
00:05:09,515 --> 00:05:12,068
Wet the plate and lay the stone on it.

67
00:05:12,068 --> 00:05:21,202
Work in overlapping circles or figures of eight with even pressure, and rotate the stone every so often so one end is not always loaded.

68
00:05:21,202 --> 00:05:23,553
Rinse the slurry away as it builds.

69
00:05:23,553 --> 00:05:29,127
Trapped slurry slows the cut, and loose silicon carbide is hard on a diamond plate.

70
00:05:29,827 --> 00:05:31,979
This trick makes the job visible.

71
00:05:31,979 --> 00:05:35,631
First, draw a pencil grid across the whole working face.

72
00:05:35,631 --> 00:05:37,001
Second, lap and look.

73
00:05:37,001 --> 00:05:42,544
The high ends are cut first and lose their marks, while the hollow middle keeps them.

74
00:05:42,544 --> 00:05:46,978
Third, when every square has gone, the whole face is level, so stop.

75
00:05:46,978 --> 00:05:49,652
Work the whole face, not just the middle.

76
00:05:49,652 --> 00:05:55,195
That is the instinct, and it is exactly wrong, because the middle is already too low.

77
00:05:55,195 --> 00:05:59,955
Check with the straight edge as you go and stop the moment it reads flat.

78
00:05:59,955 --> 00:06:02,694
Every pass costs stone you never get back.

79
00:06:02,694 --> 00:06:08,171
If a block is heavily worn, a workshop with a surface grinder is a fair alternative.

80
00:06:08,871 --> 00:06:15,754
Follow whatever guidance came with your stone, because different blocks are made to be used differently.

81
00:06:15,754 --> 00:06:19,460
A stone meant for dry use does not improve with soaking.

82
00:06:19,460 --> 00:06:22,504
Whatever you own, the essentials are the same.

83
00:06:22,504 --> 00:06:31,636
Keep the face clean and flat, wipe the steel dust away as it appears, and make sure the stone is bone dry before it goes back in your bag.

84
00:06:31,636 --> 00:06:36,268
Do not improvise with oil on a stone that was not sold as an oilstone.

85
00:06:36,268 --> 00:06:40,305
Oil holds metal in the pores instead of letting it rinse out.

86
00:06:40,305 --> 00:06:46,658
A couple of sessions can turn a stone you could have rinsed clean into one that needs degreaser.

87
00:06:46,658 --> 00:06:52,018
Plain water, or water with a drop of soap, is all most speed skating stones want.

88
00:06:52,718 --> 00:06:55,234
A mildly dished stone is worth saving.

89
00:06:55,234 --> 00:07:05,891
Replace it instead when the block is thin enough to flex or sit unstable under your hand, when it has cracked, or when flattening would eat most of what is left.

90
00:07:05,891 --> 00:07:10,790
A stone too thin to stay rigid follows the blade instead of correcting it.

91
00:07:10,790 --> 00:07:13,239
Check both faces while you are at it.

92
00:07:13,239 --> 00:07:21,381
The two sides of a combination stone wear separately, so the coarse side you rarely use can be dished without you noticing.

93
00:07:21,381 --> 00:07:24,691
In a warm, humid climate, also watch for moisture.

94
00:07:24,691 --> 00:07:29,259
A damp stone in a closed kit bag means rust on everything it touched.

95
00:07:29,259 --> 00:07:34,819
Dry everything, store the stone flat, and keep it apart from your blades if you can.

96
00:07:35,519 --> 00:07:39,397
Every edge you own is a copy of the surface that made it.

97
00:07:39,397 --> 00:07:41,574
Keep the stone clean so it cuts.

98
00:07:41,574 --> 00:07:43,683
Keep it flat so it cuts square.

99
00:07:43,683 --> 00:07:50,623
Dry it before it goes away, and check both faces often enough to find a problem before your blades do.

100
00:07:50,623 --> 00:08:00,080
The full guide on the LiveLica blog also covers caring for your deburr stone, polishing triangle and jig, plus answers to common questions.

101
00:08:00,080 --> 00:08:02,733
Find it at the link in the description.
